 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Allows a prescription for a schedule II, III or IV controlled substance to be partially filled at the request of the prescriber or patient.",
 "title": "Relates to the dispensing of partially filled prescriptions",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A3918",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A3918-2019",
     "printNo": "A3918",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 151\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 3918, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the public health law and the education law, in\n      relation to the dispensing of partially filled prescriptions\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill provides that at  the  request  of  the  prescriber  or  the\npatient,  a  prescription  for  a  Schedule  II,  III  or IV drug may be\npartially filled within 30  days  from  the  date  of  issuance  of  the\nprescription.    The bill would also allow the remaining quantity of the\nprescription to be dispensed separately within the same 30 days.\n \n  Public Health Law currently  allows  the  Commissioner  of  Health  to\npromulgate  regulations  allowing  a  pharmacist  to  dispense a part or\nportion of  a  prescription  for  a  controlled  substance.  Regulations\nalready  permit  a  patient to request, and a pharmacy to dispense, less\nthan the prescribed amount for  a  Schedule  III,  IV  or  V  controlled\nsubstance  provided  that:  (1)  each partial filling is recorded in the\nsame manner as a refill; and (2) the total quantity dispensed  does  not\nexceed the total quantity prescribed for a 30-day period.\n \n  While  the  bill  would  codify some of these existing regulations, as\ndrafted this legislation would  create  unintended  gaps  for  patients.\nSpecifically,  the bill would eliminate a patient's ability to request a\npartial fill for Schedule V controlled substances  under  current  State\nregulations.  The  bill  would also limit patients' ability to a partial\nrefill of a Schedule III or IV controlled substance  by  requiring  that\nthe  remaining  quantity of a partially filled prescription be dispensed\nseparately within 30-days from the date the prescription was  originally\nissued.\n \n  Finally,  the bill would authorize conduct currently prohibited by the\nfederal government by allowing a prescriber  or  patient  to  request  a\npartial  fill  of  a Schedule II controlled substance. While the federal\ngovernment amended the Controlled Substances Act in 2016  to  allow  for\nthe  partial  dispensing of a Schedule II controlled substance, the U.S.\nDrug Enforcement Administration has not yet updated its regulations  and\nguidance to permit partial fills.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
